From: Majid Arasskhani <M.Arasskhani@gosilkroad.net>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 13:43:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/10] fix(chat): settle cancelled/disconnected streams; stop
 consuming upstream on Stop

Two related defects in stream_chat's shutdown path:

1. Settlement was skipped on client disconnect. Starlette cancels a
   StreamingResponse body via an anyio cancel scope, and anyio re-delivers
   CancelledError at every following await until the scope exits. The
   finalizer's first await (heartbeat gather / asyncio.shield(...)) was
   therefore re-cancelled and persister.finalize, memory-usage recording,
   _persist_stream_usage (settle + RequestLog) and finalize_agent_run never
   ran: the hold stayed 'held' until TTL (default 2h) and consumed tokens were
   never charged. asyncio.shield protects the inner coroutine, not the frame.
   The whole finalizer now runs inside anyio.CancelScope(shield=True); the
   trailing metadata/[DONE] yields stay outside it and are suppressed once the
   consumer is gone (yielding from a cancelled or closing generator either
   swallows the cancellation or raises 'async generator ignored GeneratorExit').
   GeneratorExit (server-side aclose) is handled explicitly so that path is
   settled too. finish_metered_usage gets the same frame-level shield.

2. Stop did not interrupt the provider stream. client_disconnected only muted
   yields; the loop drained the upstream generator to its natural end and the
   full completion was billed. The loop now breaks as soon as the disconnect
   is detected and best-effort closes the LiteLLM stream wrapper
   (_close_upstream_stream); the existing post-loop code estimates tokens for
   what was received so the partial turn is settled with cost_source=estimated.

Tests (tests/test_stream_cancellation_settles.py) drive stream_chat under a
real anyio task group cancel, through agen.aclose(), and through
request.is_disconnected(); on the previous code they fail with
'settlement must run once even when cancelled', 'async generator ignored
GeneratorExit' and an infinite provider drain respectively.

